.\" vmxplore.1 — manual page for vmxplore, the KVM console.
.\" Format: mdoc(7). Render check: mandoc -Tlint docs/vmxplore.1
.Dd August 9, 2026
.Dt VMXPLORE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vmxplore ,
.Nm vmx
.Nd KVM and libvirt console for Linux
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -tui
.Op Fl -once
.Op Fl -connect Ar dest
.Op Fl -rules Ar file
.Op Fl -version
.Nm
.Fl -appliances
.Nm
.Fl -appliance Ar name Fl -vm Ar vmname
.Op Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Nm
.Fl -appliance-script Ar name
.Op Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a console for the KVM hypervisor your kernel already has.
It reads the estate through
.Xr libvirt 3 ,
joins it to the ZFS volumes underneath when there are any, and drives
everything with plain
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr zfs 8
and
.Xr virt-install 1
commands.
Nothing is hidden and nothing is invented: every mutation prints the exact
command it runs, and every executed command is appended to an audit log
.Pq see Sx FILES .
.Pp
Two surfaces share one engine:
.Bl -tag -width "vmxplore --tui"
.It Nm
the native GUI.
In the static terminal-only build this starts the TUI instead.
.It Nm Fl -tui
the terminal UI, for headless hosts and SSH sessions.
.El
.Pp
The tool is tiered by what the host can do, by probe and never by licence
check.
Stock Linux with KVM gives the full console; adding OpenZFS unlocks clone
lineage, golden images and rollback; a
.Lk https://kldload.com kldload
host additionally exposes its own toolset.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "--appliance-script name"
.It Fl -tui
Start the terminal UI.
.It Fl -once
Print the estate table once and exit.
Intended for scripts, cron and smoke tests.
.It Fl -connect Ar dest
Drive a remote hypervisor.
.Ar dest
may be a host name, a
.Ar user Ns @ Ns Ar host
pair, or a full libvirt URI such as
.Pa qemu+ssh://host/system .
Estate, verbs, the ZFS join and both consoles all follow, authenticated
with the same SSH key as your shell.
No agent is installed on the far side.
.It Fl -rules Ar file
Grouping and classification rules.
Defaults to
.Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules
when present, otherwise a built-in profile.
.It Fl -appliances
List the Apps catalog with each entry's fields, then exit.
.It Fl -appliance Ar name
Build catalog entry
.Ar name
as a VM and exit.
Options describe the guest;
.Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value
pairs configure the application.
By default it waits for the first boot to finish and prints the
appliance's real URL on standard output.
.It Fl -appliance-script Ar name
Print the generated installer for
.Ar name
and exit, without building anything.
The result is ordinary
.Xr bash 1
with no dependency on
.Nm ,
libvirt or ZFS.
.It Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Pp
These accompany
.Fl -appliance :
.Bl -tag -width "--password pass"
.It Fl -vm Ar name
The VM's name.
Required.
.It Fl -user Ar user
Guest login.
Defaults to
.Ar admin .
.It Fl -password Ar pass
Guest password.
.It Fl -ssh-key Ar file
Public key file to install for the guest login.
.It Fl -no-wait
Return as soon as the VM is defined, without waiting for its first boot.
.El
.Sh THE ESTATE
The left pane is the estate: every domain libvirt knows about, joined to
the storage underneath it.
.Pp
Domains are grouped into collapsible branches by the ruleset
.Pq see Sx RULES .
A branch header carries its member count and how many are running; groups
with nothing running are folded on first paint and never fought with
afterwards.
Each leaf carries two lines: the domain name and its state, then vCPU
count, memory, guest address, backing volume and snapshot count.
.Pp
State is colour as well as text — green for running, dull brown for shut
off, amber for warned, white for paused.
.Bl -tag -width "ctrl-click"
.It Ic click
Select a domain.
The console pane, the dossier and the verb row all follow it.
.It Ic click No on the state dot
Check the row for a batch operation.
A bar appears above the list once anything is checked.
.It Ic shift-click , Ic ctrl-click
Extend the checked range from the last one toggled.
.It Ic right-click
The full verb menu for that domain.
.El
.Pp
The batch bar runs Start, Reboot, Shut down, Force off or Delete across
every checked domain.
Failures are collected per domain rather than aborting the sweep, so one
bad machine cannot stop a run of forty.
.Pp
The search box filters the tree as you type; branches with no surviving
member disappear with their members.
.Sh THE CONSOLE
The right pane carries five tabs, in the order of the work — look at the
machine you have, then make one, with the substrate's toolset last.
.Ss Serial
A real terminal attached to
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm console
for the selected domain, inside the window.
Boot messages, a login prompt, a headless tty, copy and paste.
It follows the selection automatically: attaching when a running domain is
picked, swapping when the selection changes, detaching when it stops.
.Ss Screen
The guest's framebuffer, rendered by a from-scratch RFB (VNC) client
directly into the window.
There is no
.Xr websockify 1 ,
no noVNC and no external viewer anywhere in the path — the client speaks
to qemu's VNC server over loopback, or over the SSH transport when the
target is remote.
.Pp
Mouse and keyboard are forwarded, including modifiers; pointer position is
mapped through the letterbox so clicks land where they appear to.
The guest's own clipboard changes arrive on the host clipboard.
.Ss Apps
The catalog: a self-hosted application delivered as a configured machine.
See
.Sx APPS .
.Ss VM
The builders: New VM, Application, EZ Fleet, Clone and Make Golden, as a
grid of tiles.
See
.Sx BUILDING MACHINES .
.Ss kldload
On a kldload host, a launcher for the substrate's own tools, each running
in an embedded terminal in the pane.
Tools are coloured by what they do: green builds, blue is storage, gold
reads, red destroys, purple is a guided demo.
Selecting a tool opens a second page of its verbs, curated from that
tool's real usage banner rather than guessed.
.Pp
On any other host the tab explains what a kldload substrate adds.
.Ss Console controls
.Bl -tag -width "the paste button"
.It Ic \(sh
Hand the selected pane the whole window, with no tab bar, no card border
and no window padding.
The restore control floats in the top-right corner and is the only way
back — a key chord cannot be, because the guest owns the keyboard and
anything that wants
.Ic Escape
would swallow it.
.It Ic paste
Send the host clipboard to the guest.
See
.Sx CLIPBOARD .
.El
.Sh VERBS
Every mutation is a plan: a title, an exact list of commands, and a
warning when there is a consequence worth stating.
Plans execute in order and stop at the first failure.
The command appears in the status line as it runs and is appended to the
audit log
.Pq see Sx FILES .
.Pp
There is no confirmation step on any verb, including
.Cm delete .
A VM here is made from a golden in seconds and remade as fast; a gate that
taxes every real deletion to insure against a rare mistaken one is the
wrong trade.
The audit log is the record that replaced it.
.Ss Lifecycle
.Bl -tag -width "Force off"
.It Cm Start
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm start .
Refuses a domain that is already running.
.It Cm Shut down
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm shutdown
— a graceful ACPI request the guest may take its time over.
.It Cm Force off
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm destroy
— the plug pull, with no chance for the guest to flush.
Refused for transient domains, where
.Cm destroy
does not stop the machine but erases its definition.
.It Cm Reboot , Cm Suspend , Cm Resume
The light lifecycle verbs, each refusing a domain in the wrong state.
.It Cm Delete
Forces the domain off if it is running, undefines it including its NVRAM,
and destroys the backing volume with every snapshot and clone under it.
A transient domain is not undefined, because
.Cm destroy
already erased it and the failed
.Cm undefine
would abort the plan before the volume was removed.
.El
.Ss Storage
.Bl -tag -width "Rollback..."
.It Cm Snapshot ...
A ZFS snapshot of the backing volume, named
.Pa manual- Ns Ar suffix ,
defaulting to a timestamp.
This is a storage snapshot, not
.Xr virsh 1
.Cm snapshot-create :
the volume is the truth.
A running guest is warned that the result is crash-consistent.
.It Cm Rollback ...
.Xr zfs 8
.Cm rollback Fl r
to a chosen snapshot, newest first.
Refuses while the domain runs, and states how many newer snapshots
.Fl r
will destroy.
.El
.Ss Configure
.Bl -tag -width "Autostart on/off"
.It Cm vCPU / memory ...
Sets both through
.Fl -config ,
so the change lands in the persistent definition and takes effect on the
next start.
Safe for a running domain, and the only variant that survives a reboot.
.It Cm Autostart on/off
Whether the domain starts with the host.
.El
.Ss Estate
.Bl -tag -width "Migrate to host..."
.It Cm Migrate to host ...
Live migration with storage.
Designed, not yet built.
.El
.Sh BUILDING MACHINES
.Ss New VM
Two sources, chosen in the dialog:
.Bl -tag -width "installer ISO"
.It Cm cloud image
A distribution's own cloud image, configured by cloud-init and booting
ready to use.
Presets cover
.Ar fedora ,
.Ar debian ,
.Ar ubuntu ,
.Ar centos ,
.Ar rocky
and
.Ar arch ,
or a path to any qcow2 you already have.
Images are cached — the family's caches are searched first, so a host
never downloads the same image twice.
.It Cm installer ISO
Any installer image, booted the ordinary way with the distribution's own
installer in the Screen tab.
An Arch live ISO or a RHEL DVD work exactly as they would on metal.
Nothing is special-cased.
.El
.Pp
The dialog also collects vCPU count, memory, disk size, and — in cloud
mode — the guest login.
.Pp
Storage is a sparse ZFS volume when the host has a pool, with the parent
dataset derived from where the existing estate already lives.
Without ZFS the pipeline falls back to a qcow2 file, and everything else
behaves identically.
.Pp
The machine is defined persistently before the dialog closes: a domain
that exists only until the host reboots is a trap, not a VM.
.Ss The guest login
A named account with passwordless
.Xr sudo 8 ,
a shell, and whichever of a password or an SSH public key was supplied.
The key field is prefilled from the operator's own
.Pa ~/.ssh
when a public key can be found.
.Pp
If neither a password nor a key is given, a default password is set
anyway.
An account with neither is unusable at the console
.Em and
over SSH, and a machine nobody can log into is a machine that can only be
destroyed.
.Ss Post-install scripts
Anything pasted into the post-install box is written into the guest and
run once, as root, on its first boot.
Output goes to the guest's cloud-init log and to both consoles, so the
build narrates itself rather than sitting on a login prompt.
.Pp
This is how a stock image becomes somebody's own appliance: cloud image,
plus a script, then Make Golden, then clone.
.Ss Make Golden
Seals a machine into a template:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
shut it down and wait for it,
.It
generalise it — the substrate's own sealer when present, otherwise
.Xr virt-sysprep 1 ,
otherwise a loud warning that neither was found,
.It
snapshot the volume as
.Pa @golden .
.El
.Pp
Re-sealing is refused while clones still depend on the existing golden,
and the dependants are named.
.Ss Clone
A zero-copy ZFS clone: snapshot the source volume, clone the snapshot, and
register the new domain with
.Xr virt-clone 1
.Fl -preserve-data
so it gets a fresh UUID and MAC addresses over the same blocks.
When the source has a
.Pa @golden ,
that snapshot is used directly and no new one is taken.
.Pp
Blocks are shared until a clone diverges, so a clone costs almost nothing
until it is used.
.Ss EZ Fleet
One dialog for the whole pattern: build a machine, let it settle, seal it
into a golden, then stamp out a chosen number of clones.
Cloud mode only — a golden must be a finished system — and it needs a ZFS
parent.
.Ss GPUs
The host's display controllers are probed from sysfs, and over the SSH
transport when the target is remote: the card that matters is the one
attached to the machines, not the one in the laptop driving them.
.Pp
When an NVIDIA card is present, New VM offers to install its drivers in
the guest — the generic kernel with matching headers for DKMS, the
non-free component enabled, and one reboot into it.
The option does not appear on a host without one.
.Pp
Installing the driver is the guest half.
Handing the card to the guest is host-side
.Xr vfio 4
passthrough, which
.Nm
does not do; passthrough is exclusive, so on a single-GPU desktop the
host's display goes with it.
.Sh APPS
An entry in the catalog is data, not code: a cloud-image preset, a sizing
default, a handful of operator-facing fields, and a fixed
.Xr bash 1
post-installer.
Adding an application touches neither the pipeline, the GUI, nor the
tests.
.Pp
Pick an entry, answer its questions, and the ordinary New VM pipeline
builds it — then waits until the application answers on its port and
prints the URL it actually reached.
.Pp
Fields marked as generated are filled from
crypto/rand
when left blank, so the happy path needs no typing and no reused password.
Secret fields are masked in the interface; they are necessarily in clear
in the rendered script, which is written into the guest.
.Pp
Operator input is
.Em never
interpolated into the body of a script.
The body is fixed bash that reads named variables, and only shell-quoted
assignments are prepended — a site name containing a quote, a
.Ql $(...)
or a backtick is inert data.
Values containing a newline are rejected, because the scripts write them
into line-oriented configuration.
.Pp
.Fl -appliance-script
prints the rendered installer without building anything.
The result has no dependency on
.Nm ,
libvirt or ZFS: it is an ordinary script an upstream project could publish
as its own install page, and it lets you read exactly what the button is
about to run.
.Sh REMOTE
.Fl -connect
points the whole tool at another hypervisor.
The estate read, every verb, the ZFS join, the serial console and the
graphical console all follow, authenticated with the same SSH key as your
shell.
Nothing is installed on the far side.
.Pp
Only the ZFS mutations are elevated, and only locally; the libvirt calls
are not wrapped in
.Xr sudo 8 ,
because running them as root on a remote target would use root's SSH keys
and a connection the operator never authorised.
.Sh CLIPBOARD
Copying inside a guest puts the text on the host clipboard.
.Pp
Pasting into a guest sets the guest's clipboard and sends it one
.Ic ctrl-V ,
so the guest performs its own paste: atomic, and correct for its keyboard
layout.
Every machine
.Nm
builds is given the virtio-serial channel this needs, and appliances
install the guest agent that reads it.
.Pp
An imported image with no agent will ignore the channel.
A paste that does nothing is recoverable; a paste that inserts mangled
text is not.
.Sh RULES
A rules file classifies snapshots and groups domains.
Lines are:
.Bl -tag -width "group <regex> <label>"
.It Cm snap noise Ar prefix
Snapshots with this prefix are automation, and are kept out of the
operator-visible count.
.It Cm group Ar regex Ar label
Domains matching
.Ar regex
are collected under
.Ar label .
.El
.Pp
Without a site naming convention every domain is its own story, so the
default profile groups nothing and everything lands in
.Ar ungrouped .
Copy the built-in profile to
.Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules
and add
.Cm group
lines to teach it your convention.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.local/state/vmxplore/vmx.log"
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/vmx.log
Audit log: when, who, the exact command, and its exit code.
.It Pa ~/.local/state/vmxplore/vmx.log
The same, when the system path is not writable.
.It Pa /etc/vmxplore/rules
Grouping and classification rules.
.It Pa /var/lib/vmxplore/images
Downloaded cloud images.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Everyday
Print the estate once, for a script or a cron check:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --once
.Ed
.Pp
Drive a headless hypervisor over SSH, with both consoles following:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --connect qemu+ssh://fiend.example.net/system
vmx --connect user@fiend.example.net
vmx --connect fiend
.Ed
.Pp
List the catalog with every entry's fields, then build one and wait for
its URL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --appliances
vmx --appliance "WriteFreely" --vm blog \e
    WF_SITE_NAME="My Blog" WF_ADMIN_USER=matt
.Ed
.Pp
Read the installer an entry would run, without building anything:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vmx --appliance-script "WriteFreely" > writefreely-install.sh
.Ed
.Ss Post-install scripts
Anything in the post-install box is written into the guest and run once,
as root, on the first boot.
Output lands in the guest's cloud-init log and on both consoles, so you
can watch it work.
.Pp
Start every one of them the same way.
.Sy set -Eeuo pipefail
means a failed step stops the script instead of quietly leaving a
half-built machine that looks finished:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Packages and a shell you can live in .
The guest login is the account you named in the dialog; the script runs as
root, so anything written into a home directory has to be given back to
its owner:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \e
    vim tmux git curl htop ripgrep jq rsync

u=admin                          # the guest login from the dialog
h=$(getent passwd "$u" | cut -d: -f6)

cat >>"$h/.bashrc" <<'RC'
# --- added at first boot -------------------------------------------
export EDITOR=vim
export HISTSIZE=100000 HISTFILESIZE=100000
export HISTCONTROL=ignoreboth:erasedups
shopt -s histappend checkwinsize

alias ll='ls -alh --color=auto'
alias ..='cd ..'
alias g=git
PS1='\eu@\eh:\ew\e$ '
RC

cat >"$h/.vimrc" <<'VIM'
set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on
set number relativenumber
set expandtab shiftwidth=4 tabstop=4
set ignorecase smartcase incsearch hlsearch
set undofile undodir=~/.vim/undo
set mouse=a
VIM

install -d -o "$u" -g "$u" "$h/.vim/undo"
chown "$u:$u" "$h/.bashrc" "$h/.vimrc"
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy A web server .
Install it, write a page, and leave the service enabled so the machine
comes back serving after a reboot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends nginx

cat >/var/www/html/index.html <<'HTML'
<!doctype html>
<title>it works</title>
<h1>built on first boot</h1>
HTML

systemctl enable --now nginx
echo "serving on http://$(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}')/"
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy A container host .
Docker's own repository, because a distribution's package is usually
older than the documentation people will follow:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg

install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/debian/gpg |
    gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg

\&. /etc/os-release
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg]" \e
     "https://download.docker.com/linux/debian $VERSION_CODENAME stable" \e
     >/etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list

apt-get update
apt-get install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io \e
    docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
usermod -aG docker admin
systemctl enable --now docker
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy A development box .
Toolchains that move faster than the distribution ships them are worth
pinning by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

GO_VERSION=1.26.0

export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \e
    build-essential git curl ca-certificates

tar_name="go${GO_VERSION}.linux-amd64.tar.gz"
curl -fsSLO "https://go.dev/dl/${tar_name}"
rm -rf /usr/local/go
tar -C /usr/local -xzf "$tar_name"
rm -f "$tar_name"

cat >/etc/profile.d/go.sh <<'SH'
export PATH=$PATH:/usr/local/go/bin:$HOME/go/bin
SH
chmod 0644 /etc/profile.d/go.sh
/usr/local/go/bin/go version
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Locking SSH down .
Only ever do this when a key was supplied in the dialog — turning off
password authentication on a machine whose only credential is a password
locks you out of your own VM:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -Eeuo pipefail

u=admin
if [ -s "/home/$u/.ssh/authorized_keys" ]; then
    cat >/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/90-hardening.conf <<'CONF'
PasswordAuthentication no
PermitRootLogin no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no
CONF
    systemctl restart ssh
    echo "password auth disabled; key auth only"
else
    echo "WARNING: no authorized_keys — leaving password auth ON" >&2
fi
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Making the result reproducible .
End a script by recording what it built.
The next person to open the machine — including you, months later — reads
this before they read the script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cat >/etc/motd <<EOF
Built by vmxplore on $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ)
  role     : web front end
  installed: nginx, vim, tmux, ripgrep
  see      : /var/log/cloud-init-output.log
EOF
.Ed
.Ss From one machine to a fleet
The whole pattern, in order.
Build one machine with a post-install script, check it by hand, then seal
and stamp:
.Bl -enum
.It
Build with
.Cm New VM ,
cloud image, pasting the post-install script.
.It
Open the Screen or Serial tab and watch it install.
.It
Log in, confirm it is what you wanted, fix the script if it is not, and
rebuild.
A script is cheap to re-run; a machine you patched by hand is not
reproducible.
.It
.Cm Make Golden .
The machine is shut down, generalised and snapshotted as
.Pa @golden .
.It
.Cm Clone
as many as you need.
Each is a zero-copy ZFS clone: instant, and sharing blocks with the
golden until it diverges.
.El
.Pp
.Cm EZ Fleet
does all five in one dialog when you already trust the script.
.Ss A rules file
Copy the built-in profile and teach it your naming convention:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/vmxplore/rules

# automation that should not count as an operator snapshot
snap noise autosnap_
snap noise zfs-auto-snap
snap noise syncoid_

# the estate, grouped the way you think about it
group ^web-        web tier
group ^db-         databases
group ^k8s-(cp|w)- kubernetes
group ^build-      CI runners
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr virt-clone 1 ,
.Xr virt-install 1 ,
.Xr virt-sysprep 1 ,
.Xr libvirtd 8 ,
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zpool 8
.Pp
The sister consoles:
.Xr zxplore 1
for ZFS and
.Xr wgx 1
for WireGuard.
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
began in 2026 as the third console in the
.Em xplore
family, sharing zxplore's shape and theme.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Anthony Aq Mt admin@vmxplore.dev
.Sh CAVEATS
The Screen tab sends raw pixels.
A full frame at 2560x1440 is about 14 MB, so a remote hypervisor over a
slow link will feel it.
That is a transport property, not a graphics card one — a GPU in the guest
will not change it.
.Pp
.Cm zfs destroy Fl r
takes the dataset's snapshots with it.
Snapshot history on a destroyed volume dies with the volume; only
replication to another pool or host is a recovery path.
